# End-of-line contract. This is a tooling invariant, not a style preference:
# removing it breaks digests and makes `git status` unreliable.
#
# Under `core.autocrlf=true` (the Git for Windows default) a text file is checked
# out as CRLF while its blob stays LF. The index caches the CRLF file's stat size.
# When any tool then rewrites that file in LF, the size no longer matches and
# git's `ie_modified` fast path returns DATA_CHANGED WITHOUT comparing content:
# `git status` reports the path as modified while `git diff` and
# `git diff --cached` are both empty and `git hash-object` equals the index blob.
# Pinning `eol=lf` makes the checkout itself LF, so what tools write is what git
# expects and the phantom mark cannot appear.
#
# It also makes byte-level identity platform-independent. `computeEvaluatorDigest`
# and `computeInferenceCoreDigest` hash the bytes ON DISK, and `git archive`
# applies the `eol` attribute; with the attribute unset, the same commit yields a
# different digest on a CRLF checkout than on an LF one.
#
# The attribute governs the NEXT checkout and does not rewrite what is already on
# disk. To clear a path that `git ls-files --eol` still reports as `w/crlf`:
# delete it, `git checkout-index -f -- <path>`, then `git add <path>` to refresh
# the index's cached stat size. Plain `git checkout-index -f` on an existing file
# is a no-op, because that cached size still matches the CRLF bytes and git
# concludes the file is up to date.
#
# Measuring CRLF in Git Bash: `grep -c $'\r'` matches EVERY line, because grep
# strips the CR in text mode. Use `od -c`, or count bytes in Python.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary formats. `text=auto` already applies git's own NUL-byte heuristic, so an
# unlisted binary is normally detected and left alone; these marks pin the cases
# where that heuristic would be the only defence, because a binary run through
# EOL conversion is silent file corruption.
#
# Selection rule, in three parts, because only the first part is a measurement.
# Each block below says which part its extensions come from, so no mark is left
# looking like a measured fact when it is a prediction.
#
#   1. MEASURED IN THIS WORKTREE — every unambiguously binary extension found by
#      sweeping this tree's own files, ignored directories included.
#   2. MEASURED OUTSIDE THIS TREE — the container formats of the source snapshots
#      and of the external Madras set, which sit in a sibling `snapshots/` folder
#      and would be staged from there.
#   3. ANTICIPATED, NOT MEASURED ANYWHERE — no file on disk has these extensions
#      today. They are marked because over-declaring an absent binary extension
#      costs nothing, while under-declaring one is silent corruption of the very
#      first file staged with it, and staging is exactly when it is too late.
#
# No extension here is tracked today — that is precisely why the marks matter,
# since the attribute must already be in force the first time one is staged.

# Model and inference assets — all part 1: public/models/**/onnx,
# public/vendor/transformers-wasm, benchmark/data/checkpoints/**.
*.onnx binary
*.wasm binary
*.safetensors binary

# Archives, spanning all three parts. Part 1: .tgz, in benchmark/data/. Part 2:
# .bz2 and .zip are the source-snapshot containers and .parquet is the external
# Madras set. Part 3: .7z, .gz and .xz are on no disk here — sibling archive
# formats a future snapshot could arrive in.
*.7z binary
*.bz2 binary
*.gz binary
*.parquet binary
*.tgz binary
*.xz binary
*.zip binary

# Python bench by-products — part 1: __pycache__ under benchmark/lab, pickled
# estimators.
*.pkl binary
*.pyc binary

# Native modules and shared libraries. Part 1: `.node` and `.dylib`, in the
# dependency tree. Part 3: `.so`, the Linux counterpart of `.dylib`, absent only
# because the sweep ran on Windows and present on any Linux checkout.
*.dylib binary
*.node binary
*.so binary

# The pinned Chrome for Testing build under .cache/ — all part 1. Never
# committed, but a `git add -f` of that tree must not mangle it.
*.bin binary
*.dat binary
*.dll binary
*.exe binary
*.hyb binary
*.pak binary
*.pb binary

# Images and fonts. Part 1: `.png` and `.ttf`, today only inside the dependency
# tree. Part 3: everything else here is absent from disk, declared because this
# is a browser extension, so the first packaged icon or bundled font lands in a
# versioned path and must not be normalized on its way in.
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.jpeg binary
*.jpg binary
*.otf binary
*.png binary
*.ttf binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary

# NOT binary despite looking it: *.svg is XML, benchmark/data/candidates/**/*.raw
# are raw JSON provider responses, and *.jsonl is the corpus line format. All are
# text and must normalize to LF like everything else.
